Date: Sun, 11 Jul 93 21:24:30 PDT
From: more@chaph.usc.edu (Max More)
Subject: Ritual, Extropianism, & rationality

My main question for Paganism & ritual systems:
(1)  To what extent, and how securely,  can a Pagan practitioner engage
in rituals based on invoking or praising divine beings or forces, without
thereby eventually coming to believe -- at least to some degree -- in their
objective existence as independent entities?
 
Going by all his statements, and by my judgment of his character from
postings on other topics, Eric Raymond has, so far, avoided any belief in
Pan, or Dionysus, the Wiccan Earth/Gaia spirit, etc.  From what I have
observed (which is quite a lot), most people in Paganism, or participants
in any other system of religion-like practice and ritual, *do* come to
believe in the independent reality of the divine forces they invoke or
worship.  This phenomenon can even be observed in the case of joke
religions like Discordianism or the Church of the Sub-Genius.  From
reports I've read by insiders, many Sub-Genii have come to take much of
the elaborate joke seriously (to varying degrees).
 
Eric mentions Method acting as a comparison to Pagan ritual.  In
Method acting, the actor attempts to set aside his/her personality, so
that the desired character can be expressed without it feeling much like
*acting*.  I agree wholeheartedly with Eric that this general procedure
can be powerful in effecting self-transformation.  My objection -- or at
least question --  about Paganism (or any system invoking external
forces) is *not* to role-playing, but to the probability that *most* people
will come to accept irrational beliefs if they regularly and fully engage in
rituals and *communities* that are based on use of divine ideals.  For
another illustration:  "Moles" or sleepers, planted in "enemy" countries
so as to work into positions of influence to the benefit of the planting
country, usually develop some attachment to the system under which
they live, partly due to for so long playing the role of one who supports
the system they live in.
 
If this tendency to soak up real belief in irrational ideas really is a
common and strong one, that is a sound reason to oppose any Extropian
sanction of Paganism, or similar systems.  A few exceptionally
autonomous and strong-minded individuals like Eric may successfully
remain uncontaminated while participating, but I think the danger is to
too clear for us to recommend this particular method of introducing
ritual and developing non-rational (not irrational) aspects of our selves.
 
To make my view as clear as possible in regard to Eric:  I think some of
Eric's postings have been inappropriate, but I have no good reason to
believe he is irrational in regard to his Paganistic practice or advocacy --
I simply think he's mistaken in recommending it.  Some people appear
to be oversensitive to Eric's rather arrogant ways of stating things; rather
than insulting Eric for this, however, we would do better to argue that it
lessens his effectiveness in making his case.  Personally, I have no real
problem with discussing sex, sexual attractiveness, etc. on the list, but it
should be relevant to the main point.  (Some of Eric's mentions of sexual
episodes *were* pertinent:  Such as in illustrating how laying a role of
some mythical figure could enhance his sexual effectiveness.)
 
Since not only Eric, but other people not on this list (see Charles's Platt's
discussion to appear in EXTROPY #12 in January...) have implied that
many Extropians -- and perhaps Extropianism itself -- is a purely
intellectual system, that it devalues or denies the physical, sensual side
of life, and that it divorces mind/cognition from non-rational (not
irrational) aspects of self, I wish to clarify the issue (briefly here, and at
more length when I respond to Charles's article):
 
Since I have largely developed Extropianism, it is relevant to tell you
something about myself in order to establish that *I* am not a techno-
geek", asexual, anti-pleasure, anti-anything that isn't directly rational or
analytical.  Both Tom Morrow and I, when we putting together the first
issues of EXTROPY, would spend much time in excited conversation
about all the ideas that became the Extropian brew.  But we would also
go running, workout, go hiking, and we agreed in holding sex to be very
important.  I gain immense pleasure from listening to music (and I've
just started to practice drumming), and have had those peak emotional
experiences mentioned by Eric in response.  I go hiking all day
whenever I can find the time, and one of my favorite practices is
standing naked on the peak of a high mountain (Mt. San Jacinto in S. Cal
is good for this) -- not something associated with a dry, abstract,
intellectual Spartan.  I also enjoy going (with other Extropians in this
area) to raves -- large, underground, techno/rave dances, where
uninhibited sensual movement and expression is of the essence.  I have
studied martial arts at various times (Tang Soo Do and Judo) and see it
as thoroughly recommendable on an extropian basis.  You have only to
speak with my girlfriend and friends to discover sexually enthusiastic
and uninhibited I am.
 
Finally, I have had much experience of ritual, of non-rational mental
states, and of systems built on them.  Between the ages of 10 and 15, I
was fascinated by Occultism and the whole area of what is now called
"New Age".  I started going beyond reading about it, and started
practicing when I was initiated into Transcendental Meditation by my
Latin teacher, when I was ten years old.  Finding the videos of the
Maharishi Mahesh Yogi supremely tiresome, and having insufficient
patience to do TM really well, I moved on.  I studied the Kabbalah for a
while when I was 12 or 13, receiving lessons from the International
Order of Kabbalists in London.  Then I joined the Rosicrucians, to see
what they were saying.  This turned out to be a poor Cartesian dualistic
philosophy and some fairly boring occult aspects.  I went to only one of
the Rosicrucian rituals, involving reverberating sounds recording in the
Great Room of the Cheops pyramid (!!).  I tried other things, including
worrying my mother by preparing and carrying out a full magick ritual,
complete with fasting, ritual bathing, magic circle, invocations, etc.  A lot
of experimenting, in addition to my reading, convinced me that
practically everything in the field was self- and other-deception, mixed
together with many techniques which could produce various changes in
one's psychological states -- though generally not for the mystical
reasons given by most practitioners.  I no longer have much interest in
such things, though I meditate for thoroughly non-mystical reasons, and
I definitely like doing things like dancing naked around fires...
 
I apologize if anyone finds this personal information tedious or
annoying, but I think it helps to demonstrate that Extropianism, as I
have developed and presented it, was not the product of a monkish,
rigid, intellectual martinet.
 
Looking over the new Extropian Principles 2.5 (which I posted a couple
of days ago, but haven't yet seen in the digests), I realize that while there
is nothing to suggest anti-sensuality/low valuation of the non-rational,
there isn't much to clearly show how it is valued.  That may be
something I should improve for the next version, whenever that may be
formed.
 
ALTERNATIVE RITUAL PRACTICES
If Paganism is not something to be recommended (even though its
irrational aspects may occasionally be kept under control, what are the
alternatives?  Well, that's the topic I want to write on before long, as
mentioned previously, and which I'd like to see discussed on the list
(both alternative self-transformation rituals, and rituals for Extropian
marriages, other groups, deaths or deanimations).  For now, I will point
to my Optimal Persona visualization excercise, described in
"Technological Self-Transformation: Expanding Personal Extropy" in
EXTROPY #10.  This has similarities to Eric's invocation of a "godform"
in that it involves ceasing analytical thought (though such thought is
employed in originally constructing the Optimal Persona), and playing
the role of/identifying with a superior persona -- a persona that one
wishes to transform into.  The two major differences are:
 
        (1) The Pagan method uses pre-created roles/ideals (as does
Tarot, I-Ching, etc.).  This provides less flexibility, individuality, and
creativity than does use of a Optimal Persona ritual/meditation.  The
pre-set ideal may package together both desirable and undesirable
characteristics; the Optimal Persona is a completely individual, and
evolving persona.
 
        (2) Systems like Paganism at least require you to *pretend to
believe" in beings like Pan, Baron Samedi, Hermes, etc.  This sets up a
conflict where one part of you has to fend off intruding skeptical,
rational thoughts.  And, I have claimed, for most people the pretending
will slide into some degree of real, irrational belief.  None of this is
required by an Optimal Persona ritual since the OP is merely a
projection of one's ideals, or intended future self, and not as a non-
existent external entity.
 
This is getting long, so I'll try to be brief in responding to other postings:
 
