From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: General Assemblers

Mike Price:

> > The larger the production infrastructure, the further
> > semi-assembled material has to be moved around, at great cost in
> > failure modes and energy use.
 
> That's not true in today's economy, why should it be true in tomorrow's? 

My above statement could be wrong, I guess -- but seems obvious even
today.  Shipping costs money.  It so happens that today it costs even
more NOT to do the shipping, because of the benefits you mention of
nonlocal product and component manufacture.

> The type of economy (in manufactures, at least) you envisage seems to
> be a return to the locally sufficient communities that existed centuries
> ago.  The local blacksmith working at his forge, local farms supplying
> food.  And (almost) everybody living in grinding poverty.

Yes -- that's what I had in mind (grinding poverty).

Seriously, this is nothing like I envisage.  If you'd rather, consider
it a revolution in transportation technology, along the same lines as
canals and rail and 18-wheelers.  In the "GA World" all transportation
is done through fiber optic cable.  Today, when I purchase a hot tub
it is not delivered with water already inside.  That's because there's
lots of locally-available water and I have the technology to "complete"
the product locally more cheaply than having the water delivered.

> Product complexity in manufacturing is
> added a stage at a time, by assembly processes that are _more_ complex
> than the complexity (value) they're adding.  The finished product may
> still be more complex than any individual piece of machinery in the
> assembly process.

Well, the idea is that a self-replicating GA changes the game.  I'm
still not quite sure what it is you are arguing -- whether the GA
approach won't be the dominant manufacturing technology because
it's too difficult to achieve "general" assemblers, or because they'd
be too inefficient to compete with specialized production facilities.

The former may well be true.  I see some reasons why self-replication
could be simpler for nanodevices than for macro-scale objects, but
it's unclear how compelling this is.  It's a grand vision, but we're
barely beginning.  As for efficiency, I guess you could be right,
but I only have hand-waving to fall back on to express why I think
you're not.  If I pretend that my refrigerator is full of GAs and
raw materials instead of food, it just seems to me that when I want
a ham sandwich or a newspaper or a motorcycle I'd prefer to just
push the appropriate button, rather than order from a catalog.

I do agree that the GA will not be in the first wave of widely-used
nanotech production facilities (barring some design miracle).

I'm frustrated that there doesn't seem to be anything but intuition
to fall back on in this area.  Perhaps we should just wait and see!

