Date: Mon, 12 Jul 93 12:03:45 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: General Assemblers

While I must have missed some of the discussion between Mike Price and
Derek Zahn on this subject, I do think this is probably *THE* most
interesting question to ask about nanotechnology, having the most
impact for what a world with nanotechnology would be like.  

It would be nice to formulate a more precise statement of the
question, but intuitively we are wondering whether and economy
dominated by nanotech production technology is more like biology or
current industry.  Most biological production is very local, building
complex materials from simple components on the spot from locally
evolved specifications, while current industry is very global, with
parts traveling around the globe as value is added up to a final
product.

If nanoeconomy production is global like now, then nanotech has much
fewer policy implications, as the world may remain familiar for a long
time (and much wealth growth) to come.  If, however, production
quickly becomes much more local, great changes may follow.  There
would be little large scale trade in things, and may or may not be
much large scale trade in information.  If there is much information
trade, then the poorly understood economics of information comes to
dominate international dynamics.  If little information trade, then
the ties that keep peace between nations may be cut.

And scenarios of small reproducing colonies "taking over" the solar
system or the universe become more plausible.  If there are few
differences or scale effects in production technology, then what
matters mainly is who gets there first.

This is an issue well worth understanding better, but my intuition is
that to do so requires much more understanding of the economics of
manufacturing than I or most of the techies thinking about this
subject have.  This is one of the reasons I want to study economics.

Robin Hanson

