Date: Sat, 12 Jun 93 12:34:35 PDT
From: "Mark W. McFadden" <mwm@wwtc.timeplex.com>
Subject: Brick breaking

I received a question privately, but feel my answer might pertain to the 
PAGAN/RITUAL thread:

>SO, you can bust the 3rd brick down, and leave the top 2 unbroken?  How is this
>done?
>

 Well, I don't know how satisfying this answer will be, but:

 Do a lot of breaking. Find what works for you. I never did much of the 
hand toughening regimen, I just broke board/bricks then went on my way. I 
was most comfortable with hammer-fist and palm breaks. I never really 
learned to banish the image of my knuckles shattering, consequently, my 
punches were technically correct but "uninspired" (the fact that I was 
making my living with my hands at the time also had a great deal to do 
with it). One day my sensei put a board on top of a brick and told me to 
break the brick but leave the board alone. Which I was unable to do until 
about the tenth break. I assumed it was some sort of trick involving speed 
or something. Board flexes but brick shatters or somesuch. Started doing 
all breaks that way. It seemed to require something that breaking stacks of 
bricks didn't offer. Instead of going through the target, you deliver a 
short sharp shock. Besides, breaking multiple bricks inevitably requires 
the hand toughening regimen, which is time consuming and no picnic. One 
evening, during an impromptu private lesson, I must have given off some 
kind of signal to the sensei that that night would be particularly good for 
breaking. Instead of a board, he told me to break the second brick.And he 
did it himself to show it could be done. I guess the knowledge that it was 
possible made it easier cuz I did it the first time. As for what I *did*, I 
visualized it and did it. It's something my fist (and hips and arms and 
legs and everything else that gets involved in a blow) learned to do, I 
just stay calm and get out of the way. I'm sorry if that isn't much of an 
explanation, but anything else would be poetry.  The only analogy I can 
think of is running up stairs without watching your feet. How do you do 
that? Hell, I don't know. Practice?  
 Almost immediately (I suspect while confidence was high), he had me go for 
the third brick. I did it. Oh, there is this: when you're breaking with ki, 
( and by ki I claim nothing more than the mental model I use) you don't 
feel much impact. I did it one more time. So, sad to say, I've broken the 
third brick twice in my life. The second with some regularity since (but no 
guarantees). I'll also say this, I know when I *won't* be able to do a 
break (although I must concede that this might be a self-fulfilling 
prophecy).   
 Now I'm a married homeowner and bricks are for patios. I could break the 
first brick guaranteed, anything else would require going back into 
practice. "Glory days...yeah they'll pass you by..." 

 But....last year I was in a play at a community theatre, and we had to 
tear down the set from the previous play to build our new one. I was 
feeling good. I didn't use a hammer or crowbar all day. And it felt great.

 
  Note that this "feat" required believing in nothing more mystical than 
the possibility of breaking something without touching it. For me, the 
Eastern concept of ki or chi is useful. It might be absolutely untrue, but 
that doesn't make it any less useful. If nothing else, it supplies a 
convenient monosyllabic description of a phenomena that's been observed for 
centuries. Extropians might try looking at various rituals and practices as 
recipes rather than beliefs. Some distasteful ingredients (raw eggs, 
baker's chocolate/ shamanic drumming, invocations) with desirable results:
chocolate cake or a temporarily expanded consciousness.
 With apologies to Eric, perhaps this discussion would be easier if we 
concentrated on the rituals/exercises rather than the belief system.
