Date: Mon, 12 Jul 93 14:22:19 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PAGAN/META: I'm not the "Executioner"

I've just returned after a weekend away. I've gotten more messages urging
me to "let Eric stay" and to reconsider my verdict. And more evidence that
some of you out there are more mystical than rational--except for disdain
for "Xtianity," some of you are just as religious as any Catholic doin' the
"Vatican Rag."

I haven't had a chance to fully absorb the meaning of postings to this List
over the weekend, including Max More's articles, but folks are pressing me
to speak up (though I thought Harry S. had quite wisely urged us to table
this matter?). So here are a few comments:


1. I've received messages asking me to "reconsider" my verdict, offers to
educate me further (no thanks) in Thelema, Witchcraft, Occultism,
Spiritualism, and the like. As near as I can guess, a goodly fraction
(perhaps 10-15% of list members) are "Pagan lurkers"  that have not yet
"come out of the broom closet," as the saying goes.

2. I don't know what to make of Max More's detailed account of the              
rational aspects of "god-belief" (or whatever he called it). His         
descriptions of his own occult practices in his youth, mountain-top rituals
(naked, even), his sexual capabilities, etc., seem awfully close to E.
Raymond's. What to make of this? (And do I really care anymore about these
neo-pseudo-pagan beliefs?) 

Ironically, it may be the "hyper-rationalists" amongst us who should leave
the List. Maybe "Extropy" could be reinterpreted to its readers as
providing the "foundations" (the Extropian Principles, with a few changes)
of their increasingly popular New Age-Shamanistic beliefs. I could imagine
"Extropy" increasing in circulation dramatically, once it jettisoned those
boring science articles and instead focused strictly on personal
self-transformation (including the fast-growing subculture of "body art"
ritual mutilators..."Extropy" with a cover of a naked Eric Raymond with a
bone through his nose, leading a drumming circle in an Invocation of the
Goddess!).

3. I am tempted to add to the "anecdotal" flavor of the list with some
boasting of my own about my ability to sexually satisfy my partners, and
how Christ entered my life, how Christianity is a truly rational belief
system--transubstantiation of water into wine presaged nanotechnology,
don't you know? And let's not forget the glories of ritual cannibalism. As
I see it, the List can be smoothy turned into a forum for the conversion of
new astral travellers, reincarnationists, and Eastern mystics. Which is
just as well, as nanotech is just plain taking too long! We need to find
immortality the old-fashioned way, through worshipping a large pantheon of
gods and goddesses, wood sprites, volcano gods, and parking lot gods.      
            
                                                                               
4. I was challenged--not at my request, by the way--to make a judgment. A
simple, binary, decision. Many people commenting on this or sending me
e-mail           
seem oblivious to the terms of the challenge. A typical sort of comment to
me as been: "Who made _you_ the fucking judge of valid beliefs?"

The answer is clear: the Pan god himself made me the fucking judge!  

(Personal Anger Aside: For all you assholes out there who have been sending
me insulting e-mail about my "purge" of Eric Raymond from the List, I say
go back and read the many messages on this challenge and my acceptance of
it. Then you can drop dead because of terminal cluelessness!)
                                                        
                                                                               
5. My judgment was clear: Paganism is, on balance, not consistent with the
rational mind-set of this List, as I judge it.                        
                                                                              
For those who are trying to play word games with each and every phrase--I'm
surprised they didn' use numerology--to find "wiggle room," I say: knock it
off. I use phrases like "on balance" because no belief system is "all or
nothing"; to say otherwise is foolish. "On balance" means the overall set
of beliefs--as culled from my reading of a dozen books on Witchcraft and
Paganism, from discussions with as many witches, and from attendance at
three Pagan events. Many of them were pleasant, thougtful, etc., but their
belief in supernatural events and the whole panoply of Pagan themes is as
irrationaly as a belief that Jesus Christ was the son of God and turned
water into wine. 

I won't play the revisionist games some are playing with what "rational"
means, what "mind-set" means, etc. Nor do the semi-pseudo-Pagan beliefs
(perhaps held no longer) of Max More matter in my judgment. I was given the
challenge, not Max, not Harry, not Perry, and certainly not the List
overall. (The attempt by ESR to "poll" the list on "Should I stay?" is, to
me, a dishonorable act and one the List should not sanction--I have a hunch
some of the e-mail sent to me the last several days is a result of this
"Should I stay?" business.)

I consider the whole affair to be disruptive of the List. We did not give
flying yogis or astrology the same "fair" hearing. Not to mention Christian
miracles, reincarnation, faith healing, etc. Not that I want to see that,
obviously. And more disruption is happening as neo-Pagan lurkers rally to
the defense of the Pan god and call for censure of _me_. Calling for a poll
on what the verdict really means, and whether it should be "overturned"
(frankly, Max More's comments _can_ be seen to "overturn" my verdict, as
others have also noted. However, there is no such thing as "overturning"
the verdict in the Challenge that was given).

I was even asked to "clarify" further and I _again_ stated my verdict: not
rational and not consistent with the scientific, technophilic, rational
orientation of the List.

Witchcraft is no more valid than flying yogis (in fact, the "outreach" of
Paganism has been extended to include the "huna" religion of Hawaii
(praying to Pele to halt volcanic eruptions!), to Indian shamanism (flying
yogis), and to nearly every shamanistic, witch doctorly belief system in
the world. As long as it's not Christian, Muslim, or Jewish, it's fair game
for neo-Pagans to claim as their own. Astrology, divining, spirit contact,
etc., are all embraced. (Pagans despise Christianity with a vengeance, many
of them being "recovering Xtians." A lot of "recovering Jews," too.)

6. My verdict was met by ESR with comments like "Sigh." and "I expected
_more_ from you." This attempt to personalize the verdict, by implying I
just wasn't enlightened enough to see the truth of the Craft, to recognize
my Hidden Psychic Powers, to learn how to divine the future and communicate
with Zeus, Kali, the Great Bird, and Baphomet, was to me symptomatic of the
whole irrational flavor of Witchcraft.

7. Enforcement of the terms of the Challenge is not within my power. I was
the judge, not the executioner.It is not my job to fire up the flames and
turn the Pan god into Pan pizza. (If Perry did it, would it be "New York
style"?)

I met the terms of the Challenge. I issued my verdict: irrational and
inconsistent with the List (in extreme shorthand form). The next move is up
to the Challenger. The enforcement mechanism is honor and reputation on the
List.

8. Finally, I just can't believe that a mailing list devoted to the sorts
of things most of us are interested in (and I won't list them here) is
taking this stuff so seriously. "Extropy" is not "Fate," nor is it "Gnosis"
or "Magical Blende."

Not all lists are for all people. If Pagans, Witches, Shamans, Flying
Yogis, and New Agers are uncomfortable with slams against faith healing and
supernatural beliefs--the proximate caused of the challenge given to
me--then perhaps this list is just not appropriate for them. (Alt.pagan,
alt.magick already exist, and Eric's braggadocio about his alleged sexual
powers even has an _entire_ group devoted to similar bellows:
alt.sex.magick.)

Does anyone seriously think that the science- and technology-oriented folks
are going to remain on this list if main topics continue to be faith
healing (ESR offered to teach one "open-minded" repsondent how to heal with
the laying on of hands), astrology, psychic powers, and incredibly
braggartly and overly personal recountings of sexual experiences?

(Wait 'til you see my "Portrait of the Christian as a Young Man" essay!
It'll demolish all this Satan-inspired mysticism and illuminate all you
atheists about the *real* form of salvation, and why the only _true_
immortality comes from accepting Our Lord Jesus F. Christ.)

                                                               
--Tim May

